[#4471](../4471/) is marked **known**: one issue, one fix queued. The reports in it say otherwise.
They were merged on the subject line alone ([decision 0001](../../decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject/)),
and a subject says how a date went wrong, not why. Read by tenant, booking form and body, the
ticket holds more than one issue. Each one now has a ticket of its own.

<Callout kind="warn">
The queued parser fix closes only the parser issue. The form hint and the one-day moves keep coming in after it ships.
</Callout>

## What #4471 holds

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

| ticket | issue | in the table above |
|---|---|---|
| [support-kb#1](https://github.com/ArchieHQR/support-kb/issues/1) | The shared parser reads day-first dates month first: day and month swap. | the `parser` row, as the desk counts it |
| [support-kb#2](https://github.com/ArchieHQR/support-kb/issues/2) | The booking-v3 form prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field and reads the date day first: dates typed as told swap. | the `hint` row, "no ticket of its own" until now |
| [support-kb#3](https://github.com/ArchieHQR/support-kb/issues/3) | Dates move back one day across time zones: no swap. | the one-day moves footnoted under `parser`, counted in that row where they are filed |

## How to tell them apart

- **parser**: the tenant writes the day first, and the date came in by app, email or the
  booking-v2 form. Day and month swap: 7 April arrives as 4 July.
- **hint**: the same swap, but only through the **booking-v3** form. People type what its hint
  tells them, month first, and the form reads it day first. Fixing the parser leaves this as it
  is: the form has to print the order it reads.
- **one-day move**: no swap. The date moves back one day (Tuesday becomes Monday), or recipients
  in two time zones see two different days. The desk noted these already (`desk-notes.json`), but
  they are filed under parser by tenant. They are a time zone bug, not a date-order bug.

Intake tags each report from the reporter's browser. The tag is a guess, and the "tagged as"
column shows how often it misses. The tenant's locale and booking form decide the cause.

## Since April

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} />

## Lessons

- A subject line is a symptom. Merge on cause: tenant, form and what the body says.
- A footnote is not a ticket. If the kb's own table says "no ticket of its own", open one.
- "Known" means each cause is known, not that the reports were counted once.

